{
  "module": "Inference: prefill, decode and the cache",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "kv-cache",
    "title": "The KV cache",
    "summary": "Why decode does not redo the whole text at every step: the keys and values of earlier positions are stored and reused, which is valid because of the causal mask.",
    "covers": ["kv-cache"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "prefill-decode",
        "reason": "Prefill fills the cache and decode reuses it."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "attention-output",
        "reason": "The cached vectors are the K and V that attention reads."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "causal-mask",
        "reason": "The mask is what makes the cache valid."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "wasteful",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Each decode step adds one token, but attention for that token needs the keys and values of "
            },
            {
              "text": "every earlier token",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". If the model simply ran the whole text through again at every step, it would redo the same work over and over."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Without a cache, each step would redo the whole prefix, and the attention work would grow as n² per step."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 77)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "what-is-cached",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The "
            },
            {
              "text": "KV cache",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " stores, for "
            },
            {
              "text": "every layer",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", the "
            },
            {
              "text": "K and V of all the earlier positions",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The prompt's K and V are put there by prefill. Every generated token then adds its own."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 77)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "one-step",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "With the cache, a decode step is short:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "Compute q, k and v "
              },
              {
                "text": "only for the newest position",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Append",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": " its k and v to the cache."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Attend over the cache",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": ": the newest query is scored against all the cached keys, and the weights mix the cached values."
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "With the cache, the new token's attention costs O(n · d) per layer, instead of redoing the whole prefix."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 77)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "why-valid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Why is it safe to reuse the stored K and V? Because of the "
            },
            {
              "text": "causal mask",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". An earlier position's K and V "
            },
            {
              "text": "never depend on later tokens",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", so adding a new token can never change them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Nothing stored ever goes out of date, so it never needs recomputing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "This is a real consequence of the mask, not a shortcut that changes the answer. The cached computation gives the same result as redoing everything."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 77, 44)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "how-much",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A toy count of positions run through the layers, for a prompt of 1,000 tokens and 200 generated tokens, where each step processes one more token than the last if there is no cache:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "without a cache: 1,000 + 1,001 + … + 1,199 = 219,900 positions"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "with a cache: 1,000 for prefill, then 1 for each of 200 steps = 1,200 positions"
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "About 183 times less work. The price is memory: the cache has to be kept, which the next lesson sizes."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 77)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
